                <pb facs="0503" n="427" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg427"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">548.D1/266</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Division of Western European
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dunn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the American
                        Representative on the Committee for Refugees From Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Chamberlain</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-12-07">December 7, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Dr. Chamberlain</hi>: I desire to thank you
                    for your letter of November 26, 1935, with reference to the studies being made
                    by experts of the League of Nations concerning a possible new organization to
                    deal with refugee problems.</p>
                <p>Since the form and scope of the proposed organization are as yet quite
                    indefinite, I do not feel that the available facts are sufficient to enable us
                    to take a definite position on the question of our possible relations with
                    it.</p>
                <p>In the circumstances I should be inclined to suggest that when the discussions
                    have reached a more advanced stage you raise the question again.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James Clement
                    Dunn</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
